Analysis Context

 	Our architectural and design goals are to build a modular and maintainable system. We have chosen the Model-View-View-Model(MVVM) architecture for our system. We chose this architecture because we want a software system where the user interface and business logic are de-coupled. We now have two black boxes(Model View and View Model), where one box deals with all of the user interfacing(View Model) and the other deals with updating program state(Model View). While these encapsulations make the relationship between the user interface and business logic easier to maintain, the logic within the modules can get overwhelming and need additional structuring. 

Problem

	The parsing and handling of messages from the IBDC sensor is a core functionality of our system. Without use of a documented design pattern, the code can become cluttered and non-modular. Depending on the message received, the system will need to behave dynamically, whether that be to: prepare and send a message back to the IBDC sensor; save message data to storage; ask the phone for permissions to use GPS location. The handling of all these different cases should be done modularly and defined by a design pattern. 

Possible Design Patterns

	The nature of the problem (a state change requiring some functionality depending on the state change details i.e. the name of the message) infers a behavioral design pattern solution. The Observer Pattern is a possible solution for our problem. The Observer Pattern de-couples the logic between the event notification and the handling of the event. This pattern is modular and structures the code further, reducing dependencies and promoting extensibility. Documentation on the observer pattern can be found here -> https://refactoring.guru/design-patterns/observer. Following the specifications for the Observer Pattern as well as creating UML design documents modeling our code will lead to
an efficient and defensible software development approach to a core system functionality. 

